---
/**
 * The landing page.
 *
 * Assembled from documents, not written here: the hero strings are `copy.hero`, the column is the
 * `migration` entries the content author marked `onLanding`, the recipe is copied out of a public
 * committed file, and the long-form argument is `src/content/pages/1-landing.mdx` rendered whole.
 * No sentence on this page was written by the agent that built this template.
 *
 * ── THE ORDER, AND WHY IT IS THIS ORDER ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * §6D: "Above the fold: a column titled *what doesn't come across*, naming Windows programs
 * explicitly. This converts organisations; hiding it loses them at month two." So it is beside the
 * hero, before the argument, and on a phone it is the second thing on the page rather than the
 * ninth.
 *
 * D29 names the build console "the hero's living element", and §7 says it "replaces every
 * decorative animation impulse". A previous pass moved it up and this comment claimed it was "the
 * second thing in the fold". Measured in a browser at 1280x768 it was not: `.console-row` spanned
 * `1 / -1` inside the fold grid, so it sat BELOW both cells, and the inventory cell was 5,086px
 * tall. The console started at y=3783 — 4.9 screens down. The comment was the defect: it described
 * a DOM order, and the grid put the element somewhere else.
 *
 * ── SHAPE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The fold is now one grid with named areas and two unequal tracks, and the console is in it:
 *
 *   ┌──────────────────────┬──────────────┐
 *   │ hero                 │ inventory    │   1.5fr  |  1fr
 *   ├──────────────────────┤              │
 *   │ console + 3.5 GB     │  (spans)     │
 *   └──────────────────────┴──────────────┘
 *
 * Two constraints had to hold at once and neither gave way. §6D: the *what doesn't come across*
 * column is above the fold, named programs and all — it is in the right track, top-aligned, first
 * thing a reader sees beside the headline. D29: the console is the living element — it is directly
 * under the hero, around y=700, inside the first screen on a laptop.
 *
 * What paid for it: the landing column now shows five entries instead of seven and drops the
 * per-entry test, both of which are on /what-doesnt-come-across in full, and the first-boot figure
 * moved out of the hero to sit beside the recipe it is quoted from — which is where it belonged,
 * since `hero.firstBoot.sourceMono` and `recipeExcerpt.sourceMono` name the same file.
 *
 * ON A PHONE the two requirements cannot both be met and §6D wins. There is one column, source
 * order is hero → list → console, and the console is measured at y=3593 on a 375px viewport with
 * 2,637px of list above it. That is the deliberate cost: §6D says naming the Windows programs
 * early "converts organisations; hiding it loses them at month two", and a reader who has to
 * scroll for the console still gets there, while a reader who never sees the list leaves in month
 * two. Do not "fix" this by reordering — shorten the phone list instead, if it needs shortening.
 *
 * The rest of the page keeps its rhythm, each region a different shape (D29 item 4):
 *
 *   argument    one measure, left-aligned, in the margin-label rail
 *   recipe      full width — the file is the product — with the Marathi first boot under it
 *   safety      panel-2 1.3fr | what DOES come across 1fr — the ordering and its payoff together
 *   how small   panel-2, narrow, last
 *
 * `.stratum-section` gives the descent the vertical distance §7's terrain needs behind it; it was
 * written for this and used by nothing.
 *
 * The hero carries no stratum label. The sky layer's name in the collection IS the headline, so a
 * margin label there would print the same sentence twice; the labels start at the surface, which is
 * where the stack starts having files in it.
 */
import PageShell from "../components/PageShell.astro";
import Panel from "../components/Panel.astro";
import Prose from "../components/Prose.astro";
import WhatDoesntComeAcross from "../components/WhatDoesntComeAcross.astro";
import BuildConsole from "../components/BuildConsole.astro";
import RecipeExcerpt from "../components/RecipeExcerpt.astro";
import HowSmall from "../components/HowSmall.astro";
import StratumLabel from "../components/StratumLabel.astro";
import FactList from "../components/FactList.astro";
import { hero, site, migrationSafety, measured } from "../content/copy";
import { pageByOrder, renderEntry, PAGE_ORDER } from "../components/content";

const page = await pageByOrder(PAGE_ORDER.landing);
const { Content } = await renderEntry(page);
---

<PageShell title={`${site.name} — ${hero.headline}`} description={page.data.summary} layer="sky">
  <div class="above-the-fold stratum-section">
    <div class="hero-cell">
    <Panel tone="panel" class="hero">
      <h1 class="headline">{hero.headline}</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">{hero.standfirst}</p>
      <p class="ctas">
        <a class="cta primary" href={hero.primaryCta.href}>{hero.primaryCta.label}</a>
        <a class="cta" href={hero.secondaryCta.href}>{hero.secondaryCta.label}</a>
      </p>
      <p class="price-note">{hero.priceNoteMono}</p>
    </Panel>
    </div>

    <div class="inventory-cell">
      <WhatDoesntComeAcross
        headingLevel={2}
        columns="does-not"
        limit={5}
        showHowToCheck={false}
      />
    </div>

    <div class="console-row">
      <div class="with-margin-label">
        <StratumLabel layer="stratum-2" />
        <div class="console-body">
          <BuildConsole />

          <Panel tone="panel-2" class="measured">
            <p class="measured-label">{measured.label}</p>
            <p class="measured-big">{measured.bigMono}</p>
            <p class="measured-caption">{measured.caption}</p>
            <p class="measured-consequence-figure">{measured.consequenceMono}</p>
            <p class="measured-caption">{measured.consequence}</p>
            <p class="measured-source">
              {measured.sourceMono} ·
              <a href={measured.sourceLink.href} rel="noreferrer">{measured.sourceLink.label}</a>
            </p>
          </Panel>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="argument stratum-section">
    <div class="with-margin-label">
      <StratumLabel layer="surface" />
      <Panel tone="panel">
        <Prose>
          <Content />
        </Prose>
      </Panel>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="recipe-section stratum-section">
    <div class="with-margin-label">
      <StratumLabel layer="stratum-1" />
      <RecipeExcerpt headingLevel={2} />

      <!--
        The real first-boot message out of the public example recipe, directly under the file it is
        a line of — `hero.firstBoot.sourceMono` and `recipeExcerpt.sourceMono` are the same path, and
        the two were four screens apart. `lang="mr"` because the string is Marathi and the browser
        should say so to a screen reader and to the font stack: `src/styles/fonts.css` gates the
        75 KB Devanagari subset behind a unicode-range, so this is the only string on the site that
        fetches it. That face was 261 KB until D40 narrowed it to the characters this line uses —
        it was 267 KB of the landing page's 585 KB, and the reason `/` measured 85 against §6D's
        floor of 95 on a runner.
      -->
      <!--
        ON A PANEL, and that is §7 rather than layout preference: "All body text sits on opaque
        panels — the background never reduces text contrast."

        This figure was the one block of reading text on the landing page that was not. Bare on the
        page it inherited whatever `<html>` was painted, and once `src/terrain/parallax.ts` mounts
        it adds `.auros-terrain-active`, which makes `body` transparent and paints `<html>` with
        `--auros-terrain-deep` — bedrock, #24231F. Four paragraphs of `--ink-muted` (#5A4E40) on
        that measures **1.94:1** where WCAG AA wants 4.5.

        It was a RACE, which is why it survived: axe only sees it if the terrain has mounted before
        the audit runs. On this laptop it had not, and every local run said accessibility 100. On a
        GitHub runner it had, three times out of three, and `/` came back at 97 with four
        color-contrast failures naming this figure. The site's own contrast tool measured 1,614
        pairs and missed it too, because it reads the static cascade and this background is one
        JavaScript installs at mount.
      -->
      <Panel tone="panel" as="figure" class="first-boot">
        <figcaption class="first-boot-lead">{hero.firstBoot.lead}</figcaption>
        <blockquote class="first-boot-message" lang="mr">{hero.firstBoot.messageMono}</blockquote>
        <p class="first-boot-gloss">{hero.firstBoot.gloss}</p>
        <p class="first-boot-source">{hero.firstBoot.sourceMono}</p>
        <p class="first-boot-note">{hero.firstBoot.note}</p>
      </Panel>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="safety stratum-section">
    <Panel tone="panel-2">
      <h2 class="section-title">{migrationSafety.title}</h2>
      <FactList facts={migrationSafety.steps} ordered />
      <p class="safety-body">{migrationSafety.body}</p>
      <p class="safety-note">{migrationSafety.archiveNote}</p>
      <p class="safety-note">{migrationSafety.noBootMediaNote}</p>
    </Panel>

    <!-- The payoff for the ordering above, and the other half of §6D's pair. -->
    <div class="comes-cell">
      <WhatDoesntComeAcross headingLevel={2} columns="comes" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="small-section">
    <HowSmall headingLevel={2} />
  </div>
</PageShell>

<style>
  /* ── The fold ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Named areas, because the thing that broke here was source order not matching placement. The
     left track carries the argument and the machine; the right track is the honest list, spanning
     both rows so it starts level with the headline (§6D) while the console starts level with the
     bottom of the hero panel (D29). Source order is hero, list, console — which is also the
     one-column order on a phone, and the right one there. */
  .above-the-fold {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding-block-start: 0;
  }
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .above-the-fold {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.5fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
      grid-template-areas:
        "hero    inventory"
        "console inventory";
      align-items: start;
    }
    .hero-cell {
      grid-area: hero;
    }
    .inventory-cell {
      grid-area: inventory;
    }
    .console-row {
      grid-area: console;
      margin-top: 0.5rem;
    }
  }
  .console-row {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }
  /* The measurement sits under the console rather than beside it: in the left track there is no
     room for a second column, and 3.5 GB reading directly after the run that measured it is the
     better order anyway. */
  .console-body {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  /* ── Type. The scale exists in tokens.css and was ignored by five nearby guesses. ──────────── */
  .headline {
    font-family: var(--font-display, "Instrument Serif", Georgia, serif);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 8vw, var(--step-5, 3.75rem));
    line-height: 1.02;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    color: var(--ink, #24231f);
  }
  .standfirst {
    font-family: var(--font-ui, "IBM Plex Sans", system-ui, sans-serif);
    font-size: var(--step-1, 1.1875rem);
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    color: var(--ink, #24231f);
  }
  .section-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display, "Instrument Serif", Georgia, serif);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: var(--step-3, 2rem);
    line-height: 1.15;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    color: var(--ink, #24231f);
  }

  .ctas {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }
  .cta {
    font-family: var(--font-ui, "IBM Plex Sans", system-ui, sans-serif);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    padding: 0.55rem 0.9rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule, #8a7358);
    border-radius: 3px;
    color: var(--ink, #24231f);
    background-color: var(--panel-2, #e6e0d2);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  /*
    The primary action is the only filled element on the page. Ink on panel: 13.9:1 in light,
    11.5:1 in dark, so it is legible before it is decorative.

    It used to be marked with a 3px ore left border. An interactive border is governed by WCAG
    1.4.11 and needs 3:1 in both themes; ore on --panel-2 in dark measures 2.67:1, and
    src/components/CONVENTIONS.md §2 says as much — ore stays on the layer-boundary marks, the
    ILLUSTRATION flag and the focus ring, and nothing else. Emphasis here comes from weight.
  */
  .cta.primary {
    background-color: var(--ink, #24231f);
    border-color: var(--ink, #24231f);
    color: var(--panel, #f4f1ea);
  }
  .cta:focus-visible {
    outline: var(--focus-ring-width, 2px) solid var(--focus, #be3a12);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }
  .price-note {
    font-family: var(--font-mono, "IBM Plex Mono", ui-monospace, monospace);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-muted, #5a4e40);
  }

  /* ── The first-boot message, under the recipe it is a line of ────────────────────────────── */
  .first-boot {
    max-width: calc(var(--measure, 38rem) + 4rem);
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    /* No `border-top` and no `padding-top` here any more: this is a <Panel> now, and the panel
       already draws the hairline and owns the padding. Two borders one pixel apart is the shape
       of a rule that was moved and not removed. */
  }
  .first-boot-lead,
  .first-boot-note {
    font-family: var(--font-ui, "IBM Plex Sans", system-ui, sans-serif);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-muted, #5a4e40);
  }
  .first-boot-message {
    font-family: var(--font-display, "Instrument Serif", Georgia, serif);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: var(--step-2, 1.5rem);
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin: 0.85rem 0 0.5rem;
    padding-left: 0.85rem;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--rule, #8a7358);
    color: var(--ink, #24231f);
  }
  .first-boot-gloss {
    font-family: var(--font-ui, "IBM Plex Sans", system-ui, sans-serif);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-style: italic;
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem 0.85rem;
    color: var(--ink-muted, #5a4e40);
  }
  .first-boot-source {
    font-family: var(--font-mono, "IBM Plex Mono", ui-monospace, monospace);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.85rem 0.85rem;
    color: var(--ink-muted, #5a4e40);
  }

  /* ── The measurement beside the console ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .measured-label {
    font-family: var(--font-ui, "IBM Plex Sans", system-ui, sans-serif);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-muted, #5a4e40);
  }
  .measured-big {
    font-family: var(--font-mono, "IBM Plex Mono", ui-monospace, monospace);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-size: var(--step-4, 2.75rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0.75rem;
    color: var(--ink, #24231f);
  }
  .measured-consequence-figure {
    font-family: var(--font-mono, "IBM Plex Mono", ui-monospace, monospace);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-size: var(--step-1, 1.1875rem);
    margin: 1rem 0 0.5rem;
    padding-top: 0.75rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule, #8a7358);
    color: var(--ink, #24231f);
  }
  .measured-caption {
    font-family: var(--font-ui, "IBM Plex Sans", system-ui, sans-serif);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-muted, #5a4e40);
  }
  .measured-source {
    font-family: var(--font-mono, "IBM Plex Mono", ui-monospace, monospace);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0.85rem 0 0;
    padding-top: 0.6rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule, #8a7358);
    color: var(--ink-muted, #5a4e40);
  }

  /* ── The argument. Prose is one measure wide; the panel around it was the thing that was
     too wide, so the panel is constrained rather than the type re-capped. ─────────────────── */
  .argument :global(.panel) {
    max-width: calc(var(--measure, 38rem) + 4rem);
  }

  /* Two unequal columns: the ordering, and what survives it. */
  .safety {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .safety {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.3fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }

  .safety-body,
  .safety-note {
    font-family: var(--font-ui, "IBM Plex Sans", system-ui, sans-serif);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    max-width: 38rem;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    color: var(--ink, #24231f);
  }
  .safety-note {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--ink-muted, #5a4e40);
  }

  .small-section {
    padding-block-end: var(--space-8, 64px);
  }
  .small-section :global(.panel) {
    max-width: calc(var(--measure, 38rem) + 4rem);
  }
</style>
